window.__labLoaded({"id":"8.2","title":"Simple Linear Regression: Drawing the Best Line","goal":"Fit the least-squares line that predicts exam score from study hours (the lesson's eight students) and use it to predict the score for 6.5 hours.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Enter the data","text":"Hours studied (x) is the predictor and the exam score (y) is what we want to predict. Same eight students as in the correlation lesson.","code":"import numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nhours = np.array([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8])\nscore = np.array([48, 62, 55, 66, 63, 74, 70, 82])\n\nprint('students:', len(hours))\nprint('mean hours =', hours.mean(), ' mean score =', score.mean())","out":"","figs":[]},{"title":"Fit the line with linregress","text":"linregress finds the slope and intercept that make the squared vertical misses as small as possible. The slope is the typical gain in score for one extra hour. Its p-value tests whether the true slope is 0.","code":"fit = stats.linregress(hours, score)\n\nprint(f'slope     = {fit.slope:.3f}')\nprint(f'intercept = {fit.intercept:.3f}')\nprint(f'r         = {fit.rvalue:.3f}')\nprint(f'p-value   = {fit.pvalue:.4f}')\nprint(f'std error of slope = {fit.stderr:.3f}')","out":"","figs":[]},{"title":"Predict a new value","text":"To predict, put a new x into the line: score = intercept + slope x hours. 6.5 hours is inside the 1 to 8 hours we measured, so this is safe interpolation. Predicting for 20 hours would be extrapolation and should not be trusted.","code":"new_hours = 6.5\npredicted = fit.intercept + fit.slope * new_hours\n\nprint(f'predicted score for {new_hours} hours = {predicted:.1f}')\nprint(f'at the mean hours (4.5) the line gives {fit.intercept + fit.slope * 4.5:.1f}')","out":"","figs":[]},{"title":"Draw the data and the line","text":"The line should run through the middle of the points, passing through the point of means. The new prediction is marked with a star.","code":"x_line = np.array([0.5, 8.5])\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(6, 3.4))\nplt.scatter(hours, score, color='#1f77b4', label='students')\nplt.plot(x_line, fit.intercept + fit.slope * x_line, color='#d62728', label='fitted line')\nplt.scatter([new_hours], [predicted], marker='*', s=160, color='#2ca02c', label='prediction')\nplt.xlabel('Hours studied')\nplt.ylabel('Exam score')\nplt.legend()\nplt.show()","out":"","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"hours <- c(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8)\nscore <- c(48, 62, 55, 66, 63, 74, 70, 82)\nfit <- lm(score ~ hours)\nsummary(fit)\npredict(fit, data.frame(hours = 6.5))","expect":"Intercept 47.2143, hours (slope) 3.9524, Std. Error 0.7491, t value 5.276, Pr(>|t|) 0.00187; Multiple R-squared 0.8225; prediction at 6.5 hours = 72.9","note":"summary(lm) prints the coefficients table; the p-value for hours is the same as the p-value from linregress."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Regression > Linear (Dependent: score, Independent: hours)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / hours score.\nBEGIN DATA\n1 48  2 62  3 55  4 66  5 63  6 74  7 70  8 82\nEND DATA.\nREGRESSION\n  /STATISTICS COEFF R ANOVA\n  /DEPENDENT score\n  /METHOD=ENTER hours.","note":"In the Coefficients table, the (Constant) B is the intercept and the hours B is the slope. To predict 6.5 hours, plug it into the line by hand (47.21 + 3.952 x 6.5)."},"stata":{"code":"clear\nset obs 8\ngenerate hours = _n\ngenerate score = real(word(\"48 62 55 66 63 74 70 82\", _n))\nregress score hours\ndisplay _b[_cons] + _b[hours] * 6.5\ntwoway (scatter score hours) (lfit score hours)","note":"regress prints the coefficients table (hours is the slope, _cons the intercept); display computes the prediction for 6.5 hours. twoway draws the scatter with the fitted line."},"try":["Predict the score for 20 hours. Is the answer believable? Why is this called extrapolation?","Change the first score from 48 to 30. Predict which of slope and intercept moves more, then run it."]});
